{% extends "base.html.j2" %}
{% block title %}ISDS Thematic Watcher: a lead-generation tool for a narrow investor–State research question{% endblock %}

{% block content %}
<section class="hero">
  <div class="hero-inner">
    <p class="hero-eyebrow">An automated thematic-surveillance instrument</p>
    <h1 class="hero-title">The ISDS Thematic Watcher</h1>
    <p class="hero-thesis">
      A zero-cost, weekly monitor for investor–State dispute settlement at one precise
      doctrinal intersection: where intellectual property is asserted as a protected
      investment, a regulatory or judicial measure is the disputed conduct, and the case
      turns on jurisdiction and admissibility.
    </p>
    <div class="hero-actions">
      <a class="btn btn-primary" href="methodology.html">Read the Methodology</a>
      <a class="btn btn-ghost" href="digests/index.html">Browse the Digest Archive</a>
    </div>
    <p class="hero-meta">
      A research instrument for the Benavides ISDS project · open primary repositories,
      public institutional sources, and selected headline-level secondary feeds · runs on
      free GitHub Actions minutes.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="status-strip" aria-labelledby="status-strip-h">
  <div class="measure">
    <p class="status-strip-label" id="status-strip-h">Status</p>
    <p class="status-strip-body">
      <strong>Exploratory research prototype.</strong>
      {% if status.runs %}
      {{ status.runs }} archived run{{ '' if status.runs == 1 else 's' }};
      {{ status.screened }} candidate{{ '' if status.screened == 1 else 's' }} screened;
      {{ status.matches }} above-threshold match{{ '' if status.matches == 1 else 'es' }}.
      {% else %}
      No runs archived yet.
      {% endif %}
      Validation is preliminary. Output is machine-assisted and remains provisional
      unless individually marked human-verified &mdash;
      <a href="how-it-works.html">what that means</a>.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="band">
  <div class="measure">
    <h2 class="section-head">What it watches</h2>
    <p class="lede">
      The object of surveillance is not a word but a <em>relationship between three doctrines</em>.
      The theme was induced verbatim from three seed awards (<em>Philip Morris v. Australia</em>,
      <em>Eli Lilly v. Canada</em>, and <em>Bridgestone v. Panama</em>) and operationalised as the
      overlap of three doctrinal &ldquo;rings.&rdquo;
    </p>

    <div class="rings">
      <article class="ring-card reveal">
        <div class="ring-num">Ring&nbsp;I</div>
        <h3>IP as a protected investment</h3>
        <p>Patents, trademarks and licences, copyrights, geographical indications, data
          exclusivity, and brand value asserted as a covered investment. The question is most
          acute where the asset is informational or split across holding and operating entities.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="ring-card ring-weighted reveal">
        <div class="ring-num">Ring&nbsp;II <span class="weight-tag">weighted</span></div>
        <h3>A regulatory or judicial measure</h3>
        <p>Public-interest legislation, a domestic court judgment, or judicial conduct itself
          as the disputed conduct. A credible challenge to a court judgment reaches the
          MEDIUM band on its own.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="ring-card reveal">
        <div class="ring-num">Ring&nbsp;III</div>
        <h3>Jurisdiction &amp; admissibility</h3>
        <p>Abuse of right, treaty-shopping, foreseeability of the dispute, restructuring for
          treaty protection, and the contested definition of &ldquo;investor&rdquo; or covered
          &ldquo;investment&rdquo; &mdash; the threshold dimension anchored by <em>Philip Morris</em>,
          dispositive there and a recurring doctrinal risk across the broader seed pattern.</p>
      </article>
    </div>

    <figure class="venn-figure" id="venn-figure">
      <div class="venn-stage" id="venn-stage">
        <svg class="venn" viewBox="0 0 560 512" role="img"
             aria-label="Venn diagram of the three doctrinal rings: IP as a protected investment, a regulatory or judicial measure, and a jurisdiction and admissibility doctrine. The watched theme lives in the central three-way overlap; any two rings overlapping already reaches the HIGH band.">
          <!-- Three equal rings, symmetric; overlaps darken via mix-blend-mode -->
          <g class="venn-rings">
            <circle class="venn-ring venn-ring-1" cx="210" cy="200" r="116"
                    data-ring="1" tabindex="0" role="button"
                    aria-label="Ring one, IP as a protected investment. Select to read what it covers."/>
            <circle class="venn-ring venn-ring-2" cx="350" cy="200" r="116"
                    data-ring="2" tabindex="0" role="button"
                    aria-label="Ring two, the weighted ring, a regulatory or judicial measure. Select to read what it covers."/>
            <circle class="venn-ring venn-ring-3" cx="280" cy="321" r="116"
                    data-ring="3" tabindex="0" role="button"
                    aria-label="Ring three, jurisdiction and admissibility. Select to read what it covers."/>
          </g>
          <!-- Legible plaque + label over the central three-way overlap -->
          <circle class="venn-core-disc" cx="280" cy="240" r="34"
                  data-ring="core" tabindex="0" role="button"
                  aria-label="The watched theme: the central overlap of all three rings. Select to read the scoring rule."/>
          <text class="venn-core-label" x="280" y="236" text-anchor="middle">THEME</text>
          <text class="venn-core-sub" x="280" y="253" text-anchor="middle">three-way overlap</text>
          <!-- Ring labels, lifted clear of the circles -->
          <text class="venn-label" x="150" y="64" text-anchor="middle">IP as a protected</text>
          <text class="venn-label" x="150" y="82" text-anchor="middle">investment</text>
          <text class="venn-label" x="410" y="64" text-anchor="middle">Regulatory or</text>
          <text class="venn-label" x="410" y="82" text-anchor="middle">judicial measure</text>
          <text class="venn-label" x="280" y="478" text-anchor="middle">Jurisdiction &amp;</text>
          <text class="venn-label" x="280" y="496" text-anchor="middle">admissibility</text>
        </svg>
      </div>
      <figcaption class="venn-caption">
        The theme lives in the central intersection of all three rings. Any two rings overlapping
        already reaches the HIGH band.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>

    <!-- Description region for the figure. The default copy is present in markup so the
         section reads sensibly without JavaScript; JS rewrites it on hover/focus. -->
    <div class="venn-readout" id="venn-readout" aria-live="polite">
      <p class="venn-readout-eyebrow" id="venn-readout-eyebrow">The three rings</p>
      <p class="venn-readout-body" id="venn-readout-body">Each ring is one doctrinal
        requirement the case must touch. Point at &mdash; or tab to &mdash; a ring to read what it
        covers, or the centre to see how the rings combine into a relevance band.</p>
    </div>

    <!-- LIVE SCORING EXPLORER. The static legend (.scoring-note) is the no-JS fallback and
         always renders; the interactive control is revealed only once JS marks it live. -->
    <div class="scoring" data-scoring>
      <div class="scoring-explorer" hidden>
        <p class="scoring-explorer-head">Compose a hypothetical &mdash; which rings does the case touch?</p>
        <div class="scoring-toggles" role="group"
             aria-label="Select the doctrinal rings a hypothetical case satisfies">
          <button type="button" class="scoring-toggle" data-ring="1" aria-pressed="false">
            <span class="scoring-toggle-num">Ring&nbsp;I</span>
            <span class="scoring-toggle-name">IP as a protected investment</span>
          </button>
          <button type="button" class="scoring-toggle scoring-toggle-weighted" data-ring="2" aria-pressed="false">
            <span class="scoring-toggle-num">Ring&nbsp;II <span class="scoring-toggle-weight">weighted</span></span>
            <span class="scoring-toggle-name">A regulatory or judicial measure</span>
          </button>
          <button type="button" class="scoring-toggle" data-ring="3" aria-pressed="false">
            <span class="scoring-toggle-num">Ring&nbsp;III</span>
            <span class="scoring-toggle-name">Jurisdiction &amp; admissibility</span>
          </button>
        </div>
        <div class="scoring-result" id="scoring-result" aria-live="polite">
          <span class="badge badge-low scoring-result-badge" id="scoring-result-badge">LOW&nbsp;&lt;40</span>
          <span class="scoring-result-reason" id="scoring-result-reason">No rings selected.
            Toggle a ring to compose a hypothetical and watch the band it reaches.</span>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="scoring-note">
        <span class="badge badge-high">HIGH&nbsp;&ge;70</span> two rings intersect
        <span class="sep">·</span>
        <span class="badge badge-med">MEDIUM&nbsp;40–69</span> one strong ring with a weaker tie, or a judicial-measure case alone
        <span class="sep">·</span>
        <span class="badge badge-low">LOW&nbsp;&lt;40</span> one weak ring or none
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="band band-alt">
  <div class="measure">
    <h2 class="section-head">How it works</h2>
    <p class="lede">
      Each stage is defensive: a failing source or item is logged and skipped, never fatal.
      With no network the sources return empty; with no API key the classifier falls back to a
      deterministic keyword scorer, so a dry run works fully offline.
    </p>

    <div class="flow" role="img"
         aria-label="Six-step weekly workflow. One: gather new items from open ISDS sources. Two: skip anything already covered in an earlier week. Three: read the most promising items in full. Four: a language model rates each item against the three rings. Five: score each item and shortlist those that meet the threshold. Six: write the shortlist as an annotated digest and send it.">
      <div class="flow-step reveal"><span class="flow-k">1</span><div><strong>Gather new items</strong><br>Each week the watcher pulls the latest awards, filings, news, and scholarship from open ISDS sources: the ICSID, UNCTAD, italaw, IISD Investment Treaty News, and IAReporter repositories, plus the project's own Google Alerts and Google Scholar feeds.</div></div>
      <div class="flow-arrow" aria-hidden="true">&#8595;</div>
      <div class="flow-step reveal"><span class="flow-k">2</span><div><strong>Set aside what we have already seen</strong><br>It fetches each item courteously, following every site&rsquo;s access rules, and discards anything that appeared in an earlier week so nothing is reported twice.</div></div>
      <div class="flow-arrow" aria-hidden="true">&#8595;</div>
      <div class="flow-step reveal"><span class="flow-k">3</span><div><strong>Read the most promising in full</strong><br>Items are ranked by how closely their wording matches the project&rsquo;s doctrinal vocabulary, and the strongest are fetched so that more than a case name can be read. Where a source is paywalled, only the headline is available and the item is scored from that.</div></div>
      <div class="flow-arrow" aria-hidden="true">&#8595;</div>
      <div class="flow-step reveal"><span class="flow-k">4</span><div><strong>Judge relevance to the theme</strong><br>A language model reads each item and rates how strongly it touches the three rings: IP as a protected investment, a judicial or regulatory measure, and a jurisdictional doctrine.</div></div>
      <div class="flow-arrow" aria-hidden="true">&#8595;</div>
      <div class="flow-step reveal"><span class="flow-k">5</span><div><strong>Score and shortlist</strong><br>Each item receives a relevance score, High, Medium, or Low. Those that meet the threshold make the digest. Near-misses at or above the relevance floor of 25 are shown separately as watch-list leads, not as matches; a genuinely quiet week reports zero items and says so.</div></div>
      <div class="flow-arrow" aria-hidden="true">&#8595;</div>
      <div class="flow-step flow-end reveal"><span class="flow-k">6</span><div><strong>Write up and deliver</strong><br>The shortlist is written as an annotated bibliography, saved to a dated archive, published to this website, and emailed to the recipients.</div></div>
    </div>
    <p class="flow-caption">Figure 1. The weekly workflow, from gathering new material to delivering the digest. Every run is saved as a dated, version-controlled archive.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="band reveal">
  <div class="measure">
    <h2 class="section-head">What you receive</h2>
    <p class="lede">
      Every Monday, an <strong>annotated-bibliography digest</strong>: each surfaced development
      as a citation line, a short descriptive-and-evaluative annotation, the rings it matched,
      and &mdash; where the source text was reachable &mdash; a quoted notable line drawn from it.
      Entries that came from a paywalled feed carry no quotation, are marked headline-only, and
      were assessed from the headline alone. The same content is committed to the repository
      under a dated archive folder, browsable and citable.
    </p>
    <ul class="receive-list">
      <li><strong>A citation</strong> in Chicago form, with the source named and the URL supplied liberally.</li>
      <li><strong>An annotation</strong> that is both descriptive and evaluative, reflecting on which rings the development implicates and why it matters to the project.</li>
      <li><strong>A verbatim notable line</strong> where the source text was reachable, so the reader can proceed directly to backsourcing. Headline-only entries carry no quotation and say so.</li>
      <li><strong>A direct link to the original</strong> on every entry, with the full URL printed alongside it.</li>
    </ul>
    <div class="cta-row">
      <a class="btn btn-primary" href="digests/index.html">Open the latest digest</a>
      <a class="btn btn-ghost" href="methodology.html">See how entries are scored</a>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="closing">
  <div class="measure closing-inner">
    <div class="closing-text">
      <p class="closing-kicker">Latest run</p>
      {% if digests %}
      <h2 class="closing-title">The most recent digest is dated {{ digests[0].date }}.</h2>
      <p class="closing-sub">
        {{ digests[0].screened_str }} candidate{{ '' if digests[0].screened == 1 else 's' }} screened,
        {{ digests[0].matches_str }} match{{ '' if digests[0].matches == 1 else 'es' }},
        {{ digests[0].accepted_str }} item{{ '' if digests[0].accepted == 1 else 's' }} surfaced.
        Each item shown carries its source named and linked, and quoted where the source text
        was reachable.
      </p>
      {% else %}
      <h2 class="closing-title">No digests have been archived yet.</h2>
      <p class="closing-sub">The archive will populate after the first run.</p>
      {% endif %}
    </div>
    <div class="closing-actions">
      {% if digests %}
      <a class="btn btn-primary" href="digests/{{ digests[0].date }}.html">Read the {{ digests[0].date }} digest</a>
      {% endif %}
      <a class="btn btn-ghost" href="digests/index.html">View all runs</a>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
{% endblock %}
